<h2 id="launch-checklist">Launch checklist: from zero to first sale</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Pick one model and one niche.</strong> "Funny shirts" is not a niche; "shirts for pickleball players over 50" is. A narrow audience is cheaper to reach.</li>
  <li><strong>Validate before you build.</strong> Search the product on Etsy, Amazon and TikTok. If nobody sells it, there may be no demand. If thousands do, find your angle.</li>
  <li><strong>Start the trial and choose a free theme.</strong> Shopify's free themes are fast and mobile-friendly. Do not buy a premium theme until you have sales.</li>
  <li><strong>Add 5–15 products, not 200.</strong> Write real descriptions, answer shipping and sizing questions, and shoot clean photos. A cheap [[amazon:light-box|product photo light box]] makes a phone camera look professional.</li>
  <li><strong>Set up payments, shipping and taxes.</strong> Turn on Shopify Payments, set shipping rates (flat or calculated), and enable sales tax collection for states where you have nexus.</li>
  <li><strong>Write your policies and an About page.</strong> Returns, shipping times and contact details build trust with first-time buyers.</li>
  <li><strong>Buy a domain and remove the password page.</strong> A custom domain costs around $10–$20 a year and looks far more legitimate than a myshopify.com URL.</li>
  <li><strong>Place a test order, then promote.</strong> Tell your network, post daily on one social channel, and collect emails from day one.</li>
</ol>

[[gear:label-printer,shipping-scale,light-box|Shipping and photo gear for physical-product stores]]

<p>If you ship physical products, a thermal label printer and a postal scale pay for themselves quickly. Buying postage through Shopify Shipping gets you discounted carrier rates, and weighing at home means no more guessing at the post office.</p>

